## Current source graph and actual examples

`compute/pipeline/reflection.rs:16-55,69-129` reads parsed native variable
names for storage/texture/sampler bindings and bare struct member names for
uniforms, preserving binding/group metadata separately in `ReflectionData`
(`gpu/shader.rs:3-40`). `PassParameters` stores one `IndexMap<String,
PassParameter>` and exposes insertion, lookup, a public map and conversion
from a map (`gpu/parameters/mod.rs:5-40`). The general pass rematches member and
resource names before constructing native bindings
(`compute/pass/mod.rs:70-90,218-258,426-479`); the cached path separately
rematches buffer and uniform names (`kernel/fast.rs:234-255,316-375`). The
original primary `create_bind_groups` helper has its own lookups
(`gpu/bind_group.rs:7-35,155-159,183-187,256-258`). A scoped direct literal
search under `crates/` found only that helper's definition; it does not rule
out aliases, external/public callers, macros or other call syntax. The current
pass and cached callers are independently traced.

The source uses genuine authored shader names, an open native namespace; raw
strings alone do not establish a closed-game-enum defect. For a concrete
bounded match, the prefix-sum WGSL text declares `data`, `sums`, `count`,
`write_sums`, `pad0`, and `pad1` (`prefix_sum.rs:69-81,126-138`), while its
host `record_level` inserts those same strings (`prefix_sum.rs:272-301`). The
names' textual correspondence is source evidence, not proof of compilation or
execution. Likewise, `TraversalConfig` provides default open identifiers and
interpolates its fields into traversal source (`bvh/shaders.rs:390-490`);
`GpuBvh::bind` uses those fields as keys (`bvh/mod.rs:166-179`). The mesh
caller generates with a default-derived config and binds the default fields
(`physics/mesh.rs:183-195`), whose declared textual buffer names appear in
`physics/wgsl.rs:205-225`. The separately attributed prior
`/root/mhr_character` BVH shader report already records this relationship and
keeps native/custom namespace policy open.

## Conditional questions and existing ownership

1. **Group-qualified uniform names.** Reflection records a uniform member's
   bare name and its offset/size, without the containing uniform variable name
   or group in that member (`reflection.rs:113-129`, `shader.rs:3-8`). The
   general pass iterates groups but queries one flat parameter map using only
   that bare name (`compute/pass/mod.rs:70-90`). A hypothetical otherwise
   admitted shader with group 0 and group 1 uniform structs both containing a
   `count` member is therefore looked up using the same map key in each group;
   the map cannot express distinct values for those two `count` fields. Whether
   such a shader is valid/supported by the intended interface, whether the
   names should intentionally share values, and whether any current producer
   requires different values remain open. This is a source-level conditional,
   not a parsed shader, execution, native-error claim, or accepted unique
   finding. It refines q2331/q317's name-owner question; exact alias and policy
   belong to central reconciliation.

2. **Missing uniform policy and selection path.** `Kernel::run` compiles and
   calls general `ComputePass::dispatch` (`kernel/mod.rs:147-166`), whose
   uniform buffer starts zeroed and whose missing member lookup is skipped
   (`compute/pass/mod.rs:84-90`). A nonempty `KernelBatch::dispatch` selects
   `FastPath` when available (`kernel/mod.rs:249-286`), whose `write_uniform`
   returns an error when a member is missing (`kernel/fast.rs:316-332`). This
   is a conditional source comparison for prior successful setup and a
   nonzero dispatch; it does not establish runtime acceptance or a requirement
   that routes behave identically. The separately attributed current
   `ASSESSMENT-KERNEL-95E` already records this distinction under original
   q151's uniform-ABI/default policy. Its own type/shape and bounds questions
   remain separate from the spelling/name contract.

3. **Other existing reflection-shape questions are not new findings here.**
   The source compacts present group layouts (`reflection.rs:25-45`) but later
   indexes with the original group number and binds produced groups by vector
   enumeration (`compute/pass/mod.rs:70-80,469-479`). Original 059 q370 and
   the prior current pass/pipeline assessment already record this sparse-group
   first operation. A group's `uniform_binding`/size are also overwritten as
   additional uniforms append members (`reflection.rs:113-129`); the recovered
   873 reuse qualification and prior current assessment already record this
   multi-uniform policy case. Actual shader admission, native behavior and
   optional shape policies remain untested, and these distinct operations do
   not become aliases merely because related fixes might touch reflection.

Original 059 q317 records the retained `SEGOVB-L12` name-propagation finding
at the same original `create_bind_groups` first contract as historical
proposal 748/PAR, with original index 2274 and zero accepted-unique increment
in that proposal. This is concrete related-record evidence; it does not settle
global aliases. Original q318–320 and q340–341 are separate first-reason
records, while q151 owns uniform ABI and q370 owns sparse group position. The
prior current bind assessment's conditional Mat3 example has separate helper
and pass sites; no shared remedy alone establishes aliasing. Other located
historical shader candidates include unrelated material/native scopes and are
not promoted into this contract.

A later connected ledger decision should preserve the open authored namespace,
distinguish variable/member/group identities where supported, decide missing
uniform and type/layout admission policy at the relevant adapters, and retain
sparse/multiple-uniform and individual diagnostic operations as separate
relationships until the actual alias/repair dependency is established. Any
acceptance involving parsers, shaders, devices or runtime is deferred beyond
this source-only audit; no repair is proposed or performed here.

## Evidence and limits

`VERIFY-EVIDENCE.py` checks the exact packet file set and hashes, source
captures/registered displays, original/reconstructed queue slices, selected
historical and current records, and optional Git/transport bindings. The
separate privacy scan receipt binds the packet manifest. Three source requests
were explicitly clamped to verified EOF; their actual displayed intervals are
in `SOURCE-READ-RECEIPT.json` and `DISPLAY-QUALIFICATIONS.json`. Bounded
locators confer no body or global-absence credit. Hashes, chronological
attestations and bounded scans cannot establish comprehension, native shader
admission, supported callers, product behavior, independent custody or missing
checkpoint 3821 state.
